{"uid":"a62bee6461e60fcd","name":"test_create_single_simple_product","fullName":"tests.products.api.test_create_products#test_create_single_simple_product","historyId":"4c2ac6d91a4dc6fa3394144585f3c222","time":{"start":1795427260459,"stop":1795427260796,"duration":337},"description":"\nCreate a simple product using the minimum valid payload.\n\nThe fixture `create_valid_product` already performs:\n\n    - POST /products\n    - status code validation (201)\n    - response structure validation (Pydantic)\n    - cleanup registration\n\nTest flow:\n\n    1. Create a simple product\n    2. Fetch product via API\n    3. Verify API data matches database record\n\nProduct creation flow:\n    1. The test calls create_valid_product(...).Any keyword arguments are scenario-specific overrides.\n    2. The fixture passes those overrides to ProductBuilder in products.py.\n    3. ProductBuilder stores them and calls ProductFactory.build(...).\n    4. ProductFactory.build() fills in defaults for fields the test didn’t supply, then returns the payload\n    dictionary.The fixture passes that dictionary to ProductProvisioner to make the API request.\n\nWith no overrides, the Product factory creates data shaped like:\n\n    {\n      \"name\": \"<generated product name>\",\n      \"type\": \"simple\",\n      \"status\": \"publish\",\n      \"regular_price\": \"19.99\",\n      \"sku\": \"TEST-SKU-<unique suffix>\",\n      \"description\": \"Test product: <generated product name>\",\n      \"short_description\": \"Test product: <generated product name>\"\n    }\n\nOptional fields such as sale_price and categories are left out unless supplied. Explicit\nvalues passed by the test replace the corresponding defaults.\n\nSo product_data is the in-memory request payload; WooCommerce creates the Product\nlater, when the provisioner sends it.\n","descriptionHtml":"<p>Create a simple product using the minimum valid payload.</p>\n<p>The fixture <code>create_valid_product</code> already performs:</p>\n<pre><code>- POST /products\n- status code validation (201)\n- response structure validation (Pydantic)\n- cleanup registration\n</code></pre>\n<p>Test flow:</p>\n<pre><code>1. Create a simple product\n2. Fetch product via API\n3. Verify API data matches database record\n</code></pre>\n<p>Product creation flow:<br />\n1. The test calls create_valid_product(...).Any keyword arguments are scenario-specific overrides.<br />\n2. The fixture passes those overrides to ProductBuilder in products.py.<br />\n3. ProductBuilder stores them and calls ProductFactory.build(...).<br />\n4. ProductFactory.build() fills in defaults for fields the test didn’t supply, then returns the payload<br />\ndictionary.The fixture passes that dictionary to ProductProvisioner to make the API request.</p>\n<p>With no overrides, the Product factory creates data shaped like:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;&lt;generated product name&gt;&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;simple&quot;,\n  &quot;status&quot;: &quot;publish&quot;,\n  &quot;regular_price&quot;: &quot;19.99&quot;,\n  &quot;sku&quot;: &quot;TEST-SKU-&lt;unique suffix&gt;&quot;,\n  &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Test product: &lt;generated product name&gt;&quot;,\n  &quot;short_description&quot;: &quot;Test product: &lt;generated product name&gt;&quot;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Optional fields such as sale_price and categories are left out unless supplied. Explicit<br />\nvalues passed by the test replace the corresponding defaults.</p>\n<p>So product_data is the in-memory request payload; WooCommerce creates the Product<br />\nlater, when the provisioner sends it.</p>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"_verify_url","time":{"start":1795427259649,"stop":1795427259649,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"